"Free" means five different things in AI coding right now: a hosted model the vendor gives away, an ad-funded allowance, a weekly quota nobody will put a number on, a free harness that needs your own API key, and a limited-time promotion that can vanish on a Monday. Most roundups blur them together. This post keeps them apart and puts the limit next to every product, checked against the vendor's own pricing or docs page on November 25, 2026. Where a vendor only says "limited" or "light", the table says the number is unpublished instead of guessing one.

The short version: Freebuff is the one tool here that states a daily allowance in hours with no key and no card, though the figures come from its own comparison post. OpenCode and Kilo Code hand out rotating free hosted models. GitHub Copilot Free and Google Antigravity are the picks if you want a big vendor behind a $0 plan. Gemini CLI and Qwen Code, two names that used to top every "free" list, no longer have a free hosted tier, and Amazon Q Developer stopped taking new Free Tier signups in May, which is why Kiro is the AWS entry now.

{% youtube id="6rbd1uK_pEk" title="Freebuff: Free AI Coding Agent (Deepseek, GPT, Minimax)" /%}

*A third-party Freebuff walkthrough from the Stackagen channel (title confirmed through YouTube oEmbed on November 25, 2026). Free model lists change often, so use the limits table below for current numbers rather than the video.*

## Short answer: quick picks

- **Best free coding agent overall:** [Freebuff](https://freebuff.com). No card, no API key, and an allowance published in hours per day (up to 30 in the US, per Freebuff's own comparison post). The cost is text ads and an allowance that shrinks outside a handful of countries.
- **Best free and open source:** [OpenCode](https://opencode.ai) (MIT, 229,110 GitHub stars as of November 25, 2026). Free hosted models plus 200+ providers when you bring a key. The free list rotates and has no published request limit.
- **Best inside VS Code or JetBrains:** [Kilo Code](https://kilo.ai) (MIT). Auto Free routes to free models, and it works with your own key at no markup.
- **Best from a large vendor with a real free plan:** [GitHub Copilot Free](https://github.com/features/copilot/plans) (2,000 completions a month plus limited chat and agent use) and [Google Antigravity](https://antigravity.google/pricing) (weekly agent quota, number unpublished).
- **Best bring-your-own-key harness:** [Cline](https://cline.bot/pricing) (Apache-2.0, 71,891 stars). Free software, you pay for inference.
- **Best for trying a spec-driven workflow:** [Kiro](https://kiro.dev/pricing/), 50 credits a month on the free plan.
- **Skip for free hosted inference in 2026:** Gemini CLI and Qwen Code. Both are still free software, but their free sign-in tiers are gone.

{% callout type="warning" %}
**What changed in 2026, as of November 25.** Google moved Gemini CLI's free individual access to Antigravity CLI on June 18, 2026. Qwen Code's Qwen OAuth sign-in now requires a Plus or Pro subscription from chat.qwen.ai. Amazon Q Developer stopped accepting new Free Tier signups on May 15, 2026. Google's Antigravity pricing page says several free-plan models, including Gemini 3.8 Flash, "will be removed on November 30, 2026", five days from today. Free tiers in this market move monthly, so treat every number here as a dated snapshot.
{% /callout %}

