## Freebuff: the free tier with a published allowance

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/freebuff.png" name="Freebuff" size=40 /%}

Freebuff is a free, ad-supported coding agent from the team behind Codebuff. It runs in the terminal, as desktop apps for macOS, Windows and Linux, in a browser workspace, and on iOS and Android. The homepage pitch is "The best coding models, funded by ads instead of a subscription. No card, no credits to watch." The agents live in a public Apache-2.0 repository, CodebuffAI/freebuff, which had 14,305 GitHub stars on November 25, 2026.

{% x id="2034784332569878618" /%}

*Launch post from the @jahooma account, which posts the Freebuff announcements, March 20, 2026, verified through X syndication on November 25, 2026.*

**Who it is for:** anyone who wants an agent with hosted models and no key, card or credit meter, and who accepts text ads in exchange.

**Key features:**
- Parallel agents locally on desktop, plus a CLI, a web workspace and mobile apps.
- Work split across 9 specialized subagents, including a code reviewer (per Freebuff's comparison post).
- A model picker that recommends DeepSeek V4.1 Flash. Freebuff's October and May launch posts list other free models, so the list changes.

**Pros**
- The only entry here that states its free allowance in hours: up to 30 a day in the US, 21 in countries such as Canada, the UK and Germany, 12 in France, Spain and Italy, and 5 elsewhere. Sessions last an hour with unlimited messages inside.
- No API key and no card on the homepage.
- Open-source agents.

**Cons**
- The allowance numbers come from Freebuff's own blog, not an independent test, and I could not load the docs site during this session.
- Text ads, and messages are used to personalize ads. Only models labeled "May use data for AI training" are used for training, per the same post.
- Outside the listed countries the allowance drops to 5 hours a day.

**Benefits for you:** you can budget a working day. Hours per day is easier to plan around than "limited", and the October 9 launch post says "Sessions always refund partial usage", so a session that ends early should not burn the full hour.

{% x id="2108686918892638595" /%}

*"The New Freebuff" announcement, October 9, 2026, verified through X syndication.*

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** free. I found no paid plan on the pages read.

**Setup**

```bash
npm install -g freebuff
freebuff
```

**Standout use case:** day-long free coding sessions in the US, with the agent handling review through a separate subagent.

**Skip it if:** you cannot send code through an ad-supported service, or you live outside the 30, 21 and 12 hour countries and need a full workday of agent time.

{% figure src="https://freebuff.com/opengraph-image.png?v=2026-10-05" alt="Freebuff social preview image from the project homepage" caption="Freebuff's own social preview image. Source: freebuff.com" /%}

## OpenCode: free hosted models plus 200 providers

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/opencode.png" name="OpenCode" size=40 /%}

OpenCode is an MIT-licensed open-source coding agent. Its homepage says "Start with free models or bring any of 200+ providers" and lists a desktop app for macOS, Windows and Linux, a terminal UI, and says you can use OpenCode "from your desktop, the terminal, your phone or your own code". The repository, anomalyco/opencode, had 229,110 GitHub stars on November 25, 2026. The homepage also claims "22M+ developers", which is a vendor-reported figure I could not verify. The npm package opencode-ai was at version 1.19.6.

**Who it is for:** developers who want one open client that can switch between free hosted models, their own keys and local models.

**Pros**
- Free models need no account on Zen's free tier according to Freebuff's comparison post (OpenCode's own page only says they are free for a limited time).
- The Zen page labels several free models, among them Big Pickle, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash Free, Hy4 flash Free, MiMo-V2.6-Flash Free and Nemotron 3.5 variants. It states the data policy per model: some follow zero retention, others retain data for 30 days for safety.
- Works in VS Code and forks such as Cursor, Windsurf and VSCodium through an extension that wraps the terminal UI.
- Skills load from `.opencode/skills` and also from `.claude/skills` and `.agents/skills`, so shared SKILL.md folders work.

**Cons**
- Every free model is described as "for a limited time" with no published request cap. A model you rely on can disappear.
- Some free models are blocked in listed countries (Beam Free, for one).
- Its docs now say the current pages are for OpenCode V1 and that V2 docs live elsewhere, so check which version you run.

**Benefits for you:** the harness stays the same while the model changes. When a free model ends, you switch in `/models` and keep your skills, commands and sessions.

{% x id="2119982381021192354" /%}

*OpenCode's account announcing MiniMax M3.1 Flash as free, November 10, 2026, verified through X syndication.*

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** free client. Optional OpenCode Go: Go $10 a month, Go Plus $40 a month, Go Teams $20 per member a month with a 2-member minimum. Go is a model subscription and the page says it is optional.

**Setup**

```bash
npm i -g opencode-ai
opencode
/models        # pick a free model (inside the terminal UI)
/connect       # add a provider key or an OpenCode Go key
```

**Skip it if:** you need a fixed, published daily allowance. OpenCode does not publish one.

{% figure src="https://opencode.ai/files/site/og/home-98637499f6.png" alt="OpenCode homepage social preview image" caption="OpenCode's social preview image. Source: opencode.ai" /%}

## Kilo Code: Auto Free inside your editor

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/kilo-code.png" name="Kilo Code" size=40 /%}

Kilo Code is an MIT-licensed agent that runs as a VS Code extension, a JetBrains plugin, a CLI and a desktop app (editor and desktop coverage per Freebuff's comparison post and Kilo's pricing page). Kilo-Org/kilocode had 28,124 GitHub stars on November 25, 2026, and the CLI package @kilocode/cli was at version 7.8.32.

**Who it is for:** editor-first developers who want free models without leaving VS Code or JetBrains, with the option to use their own keys later.

**Pros**
- Kilo's free-model docs describe Auto Free (`kilo-auto/free`) as a tier that "automatically routes your requests to the best available free models". No configuration.
- You can also filter the model picker by typing `free`.
- The pricing page says "No hosted credit required" for the free path.
- Freebuff's comparison post says Kilo charges no markup on inference when you use your own key.

**Cons**
- Kilo's docs warn that Auto Free "may route your requests to providers that log prompts and outputs and use them to improve their services", and name NVIDIA's free endpoints as one example. Kilo tells users not to submit personal or confidential data.
- Some free models are rate limited by the upstream provider.
- The free model list "changes over time".
- The 200-requests-an-hour-per-IP figure for signed-out users comes from Freebuff's post, not from Kilo's pages.

**Benefits for you:** you get a real agent in your editor at $0, and a clear way up. Kilo Pass is $19 a month with up to 50% bonus credits, and Teams is $15 per user a month.

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** Free and open source (usage billed separately), pay-as-you-go through Kilo Gateway, Kilo Pass $19 a month, Teams $15 per user a month, Enterprise custom.

**Setup**

```bash
npm install -g @kilocode/cli
```

**Skip it if:** the code is private and you cannot accept prompt logging by free-model providers.

## GitHub Copilot Free: the large-vendor option

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/github-copilot-free.png" name="GitHub Copilot Free" size=40 /%}

Copilot Free is GitHub's $0 plan for individuals who do not get Copilot through an organization. All plans include the Copilot CLI and the Copilot app, and the plans page says the Free plan supports the CLI and agent mode.

**Who it is for:** developers already on GitHub who want a vendor-supported free tier across editors, the CLI, github.com and mobile.

**Pros**
- Published number: "Limited to 2000 completions per month on Copilot Free."
- Included in editors and IDEs, the CLI and GitHub including mobile; agent mode in VS Code, Visual Studio, JetBrains, Eclipse and Xcode; MCP servers; and custom instructions and agents.
- The plans page carries a "No credit card required" line in the Free block.
- GitHub's docs say agent skills work in the Copilot CLI and in agent mode in the IDEs, stored in `.github/skills`, `.claude/skills` or `.agents/skills`. The page does not say which plan tiers get them.

**Cons**
- Chat and agent use is only "limited" with an allowance of GitHub AI Credits that the page does not quantify.
- Models: Auto model selection only. No model picker on Free.
- Copilot in the cloud and most code review are paid features, and "Copilot Chat skills in IDEs" is marked not available on Free.
- App modernization for Java and .NET is not included.

**Benefits for you:** the lowest-risk free tier here. It is a mainstream vendor product with clear plan boundaries, and the paid ladder is on the same page.

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** Free $0; Pro $10 a month (1,000 base credits plus 500 flex); Pro+ $39 (3,900 base plus 3,100 flex); Max $100 (10,000 base plus 10,000 flex). Business $19 and Enterprise $39 per seat.

**Skip it if:** you want to pick the model yourself, or run long agent sessions. The free allowance is a trial-sized quota.

{% figure src="https://images.ctfassets.net/8aevphvgewt8/4KxWJH8PVVXdqBLs1V3UJ8/7b7f6e8405daa24565374f993c0fca68/github-copilot-open-graph-img.jpg" alt="GitHub Copilot social preview image from github.com/features/copilot" caption="GitHub's Copilot social preview image. Source: github.com/features/copilot" /%}

## Google Antigravity: Gemini models on a weekly quota

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/google-antigravity.png" name="Google Antigravity" size=40 /%}

Antigravity is Google's agent product, with an app, a CLI (`agy`), IDE extensions and a Python SDK. Since June 18, 2026 it is where Google's free individual coding access lives: the Gemini CLI README says free individual access and Google AI Pro and Ultra moved to Antigravity CLI that day.

**Who it is for:** developers who want Google's models at $0 and can live with a quota they cannot see in advance.

**Pros**
- The $0 plan covers the app, CLI, IDE extensions and SDK, with parallel background agents, subagents, browser use and scheduled tasks.
- Unlimited Tab completions on all plans, per the docs.
- Skills use the standard SKILL.md folder format, defaulting to `.agents/skills`.

**Cons**
- The quota is a "baseline agent quota refreshed weekly" with no published number. Google says limits track capacity and the amount of work the agent does, so easy tasks get more prompts than hard ones.
- Google's pricing page and docs disagree about the free model list (see the limits table), and the pricing page says four of the five listed models are removed on November 30, 2026.
- Workspace accounts do not get the personal plans.
- Google says usage limits "are subject to modification".

**Benefits for you:** you can test the Gemini family and a full multi-agent setup without paying, and the upgrade path to Google AI Pro raises quotas and refreshes them every 5 hours.

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** $0 for individuals. Google AI Pro and Ultra are paid tiers; their prices were not on the Antigravity pages I read.

**Skip it if:** you need predictable capacity, or you use a Google Workspace account.

{% figure src="https://antigravity.google/og/index.png" alt="Google Antigravity social preview image" caption="Antigravity's social preview image. Source: antigravity.google" /%}

## Cline: free software, bring your own key

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/cline.png" name="Cline" size=40 /%}

Cline is an Apache-2.0 agent that runs as a VS Code extension, a CLI, and extensions for JetBrains and Cursor (the pricing page lists JetBrains under Enterprise, while the docs describe a JetBrains plugin, so check current availability). cline/cline had 71,891 GitHub stars on November 25, 2026, and the npm package `cline` was at 3.0.75.

**Who it is for:** developers who want the most flexible open harness and are fine paying a provider for inference.

**Pros**
- Cline's pricing page says it is "completely free for individual developers" and you pay only for inference, with no subscription.
- Providers include Anthropic, Gemini, OpenAI, OpenRouter, AWS Bedrock, GCP Vertex, Groq, Cerebras, DeepSeek and more.
- Plan mode, an MCP marketplace and multi-root workspaces.
- Skills load on demand: metadata costs about 100 tokens per skill, and the body loads when triggered.

**Cons**
- Not free in the "hosted models included" sense. The model bill is yours.
- Freebuff's comparison post says Cline offers limited-time free model promotions up to an unpublished quota, and that Cline says free usage may be used to improve model performance. I could not confirm that on Cline's own pricing page.

**Benefits for you:** pair Cline with a free OpenRouter model or a local model and you get a $0 stack you control. Freebuff's post cites OpenRouter's `:free` models at 20 requests a minute and 50 a day, rising to 1,000 a day after $10 of credits.

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** Open Source free; Enterprise custom. Cline's docs describe ClinePass as a flat $9.99 a month subscription with 2 to 5 times the usage on popular open coding models compared with standard API rates; it does not appear on the pricing page I read.

**Setup**

```bash
npm i -g cline
```

**Skip it if:** you want hosted models with no key and no billing relationship at all.

{% figure src="https://cline.bot/cline_og_img.png" alt="Cline social preview image" caption="Cline's social preview image. Source: cline.bot" /%}

## Kiro: the AWS option now that Amazon Q Developer is closed

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/kiro.png" name="Kiro" size=40 /%}

Kiro is AWS's agentic IDE and CLI built around spec-driven development. AWS announced on April 30, 2026 that new Amazon Q Developer signups, including Free Tier accounts, were blocked starting May 15, 2026, and that Q Developer IDE plugins and paid subscriptions reach end of support on April 30, 2027. Kiro is AWS's stated replacement.

**Who it is for:** developers who want structure (requirements, design, tasks) rather than a chat box, and who had planned to use Amazon Q's free tier.

**Pros**
- A perpetual free tier of 50 credits a month.
- Free-tier users who sign in with social login or AWS Builder ID get Claude Sonnet 4.5 and open-weight models such as DeepSeek 3.2, MiniMax M2.5, GLM-5 and Qwen3 Coder Next, subject to rate limits.
- Specs, hooks, steering files, custom subagents and powers.
- Skills work from `.kiro/skills/` in a workspace and `~/.kiro/skills/` globally (Kiro's skills page lists global skills for the IDE and CLI).

**Cons**
- Credits, not requests. Kiro's FAQ defines a credit as "a unit of work in response to user prompts": simple prompts can use less than 1 credit, and a spec task typically costs more than 1.
- Different models burn credits at different rates, so Sonnet runs through 50 credits faster than Auto.
- Credits are metered per prompt and per model, so a heavy spec session can use a large share of the 50 in one sitting.

**Benefits for you:** the first upgrade to Pro gives the rest of that month free plus 500 trial credits to use within 12 months, so you can test a heavier workload without a bill.

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** Free $0 (50 credits); Pro $20; Pro+ $40; Pro Max $100; Power $200 a month. Add-on credits are $0.04 each.

**Skip it if:** you work in short chat-style edits. Spec mode spends credits quickly and 50 a month will not stretch far.

{% figure src="https://kiro.dev/images/opengraph/home.png" alt="Kiro social preview image" caption="Kiro's social preview image. Source: kiro.dev" /%}

## Codex on ChatGPT Free and Go: a small free taste of a big agent

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/codex.png" name="Codex" size=40 /%}

OpenAI includes Codex in its ChatGPT plans. The pricing page says "ChatGPT Work and Codex are included in your ChatGPT Free, Go, Plus, Pro, Business, Edu, or Enterprise plan" and that they share pricing, credits and usage limits. The Codex CLI is Apache-2.0 (openai/codex had 136,420 stars on November 25, 2026; npm `@openai/codex` was at 0.172.0, modified that same day).

**Who it is for:** anyone with a ChatGPT account who wants to try an OpenAI agent without paying.

**Pros**
- The Free plan is described as "Explore Codex capabilities on quick coding tasks": GPT-6 Luna at Standard speed in the desktop app, "subject to rollout". Go is described as lightweight coding tasks with the same model line.
- Plus adds Codex on the web, in the CLI, in the IDE extension and on iOS, plus cloud integrations such as automatic code review.
- Skills work in the ChatGPT desktop app, the Codex CLI and the IDE extension.

**Cons**
- No published Free limit. For comparison, OpenAI estimates Plus at 350 to 3,000 local GPT-6 Luna messages per five-hour period, and says those are estimates, not fixed limits.
- The CLI, IDE extension and web start at Plus on OpenAI's page, so the free path is the desktop app only.
- Image generation is not available on Free.
- "Subject to rollout" means you may not have it yet.

**Benefits for you:** a quick way to see whether OpenAI's agent fits your workflow before paying. Our own [Codex usage limits guide](https://agentskills.codes/blog/codex-usage-limits-agent-workflows) covers how those shared limits behave in long agent runs.

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** Free $0. Go and Plus prices were not on the pages I read, so I am not quoting them. Pro is $100, $200 or $500 a month.

**Skip it if:** you work in the terminal or an IDE and do not want to pay. OpenAI's page lists those surfaces from Plus up, although the CLI also accepts an OpenAI API key (billed at API rates) per Freebuff's comparison post.

## Windsurf Free: unlimited Tab, a light agent quota

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/windsurf.png" name="Windsurf" size=40 /%}

Windsurf's pricing page now sits under the "Devin Plans and Pricing" heading, and its customization docs describe a "Devin Local" agent in Devin Desktop. The Free plan is $0 and the page lists it as light quota to code with agents, limited model availability, unlimited inline edits and unlimited Tab completions.

**Who it is for:** developers who mainly want fast autocomplete and inline edits, with occasional agent runs.

**Pros**
- Unlimited Tab and inline edits on Free is a stronger autocomplete offer than most free tiers here.
- Skills, rules, plugins, MCP servers and hooks are supported in the local agent, and skills are model-invoked or user-invoked.
- Pro includes free use of SWE-2 (Windsurf's own model).

**Cons**
- The agent quota is "light" and unpublished, and model availability is limited.
- Free mode allows 2 concurrent sessions per user.
- The branding and product names are in flux (Windsurf, Devin Desktop, Devin Local), which makes docs harder to match to the app.

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** Free $0; Pro $20 a month; Max $200; Team $80. Paid plans have allowances that refresh daily and weekly, and extra usage is billed at API pricing.

**Skip it if:** you need agents more than autocomplete. The agent quota on Free is "light" with no number, so you cannot plan a task around it.

## Cursor Free: limited, and now tied to Grok models

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/cursor-free.png" name="Cursor Free" size=40 /%}

Cursor's pricing page now sells plans under The X Subscription, which bundles Cursor with Grok features. The Free plan is $0 with "limited" agent and Chat requests. Cursor's docs say Free gives access to Agent, Chat and Tab completions with Cursor models (Grok and Composer), and that the legacy Hobby plan works the same way.

**Who it is for:** developers who want to try Cursor's editor and agent without paying and are fine with Cursor's own models.

**Pros**
- A familiar full editor with agent, chat and Tab.
- Skills follow the open standard and are discovered automatically from skill directories.
- The Free plan has no trial clock: it is a standing $0 plan.

**Cons**
- No published request count: "Limited" everywhere in the plan table.
- Third-party models are not included in plan usage; on Premium and above they bill at API list price. Plus runs Cursor models at fixed settings and has no third-party models; Cloud Agents and Automations start at Premium.
- Plan names have been reshaped around X. Cursor's docs call the older Hobby plan "legacy" and say it works the same way, so older "Cursor Hobby" guides map onto Free.

**Pricing (as of November 25, 2026):** Free $0; Plus $8; Premium $30; Super $100; Ultra $200 a month; Teams from $30 per user. Premium is 4x Plus usage, Super 15x, Ultra 50x.

**Skip it if:** you want Claude or GPT models at no cost. Free does not include them.

## Gemini CLI: still open source, no longer free to use

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/gemini-cli.png" name="Gemini CLI" size=40 /%}

Gemini CLI is Google's Apache-2.0 terminal agent (109,100 GitHub stars on November 25, 2026; npm `@google/gemini-cli` 0.71.1). Its README now carries a notice that it is designed for Gemini Code Assist Standard or Enterprise licences and Code Assist for GitHub, and that there is "no free tier or Google AI plans": free individual access moved to Antigravity CLI (`agy`) on June 18, 2026. The README still lists "Sign in with Google" and Gemini API key options; I did not verify what an API key costs or whether it carries any free quota, so check Google's API pricing before assuming either. Skills are supported through an `activate_skill` flow with a consent prompt.

**Pros:** open source, mature, and skills support. **Cons:** no hosted free tier. **Skip it if:** you want free hosted models; use Antigravity CLI instead. **Pricing:** Code Assist licence prices were not on the page I read.

## Qwen Code: free software, free sign-in removed

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026/logos/qwen-code.png" name="Qwen Code" size=40 /%}

Qwen Code is an Apache-2.0 terminal agent from the Qwen team (QwenLM/qwen-code, 30,934 stars on November 25, 2026; npm `@qwen-code/qwen-code` 0.28.4). Its auth docs list five first-run options. The first, Qwen OAuth, now reads "Qwen Account with Subscription" and requires "an active Plus or Pro subscription from chat.qwen.ai". The others are QwenCloud (Token Plan or pay-as-you-go keys), Alibaba ModelStudio (Coding Plan with a weekly quota), third-party providers by API key, and a custom provider. Freebuff's comparison post says the free tier is gone and that it still works free with your own key or a local model through Ollama or vLLM.

**Pros:** Apache-2.0, many provider options, skills supported through a `/skills` panel. **Cons:** no hosted free tier any more. **Skip it if:** you were relying on the old free OAuth allowance. **Pricing:** Plus, Pro and Coding Plan prices are on Alibaba's pages, which I did not read.

