## Head-to-head: which to pick

### Freebuff vs OpenCode

Pick **Freebuff** when you want a number you can plan around. Its allowance is published in hours and the sessions are long, so you can estimate a working day, and it needs no key or card. Pick **OpenCode** when you want an open client that is not tied to one vendor's allowance. OpenCode's free models rotate and carry no cap, which is generous until the model you chose disappears. Freebuff's own comparison post lists OpenCode as the baseline it is compared against, so treat Freebuff's framing of the trade-off as vendor-authored. A reasonable setup is to run both: Freebuff for the long free session, and OpenCode with your own provider key when a free model goes away.

### GitHub Copilot Free vs Google Antigravity

Pick **Copilot Free** for predictability and for completions: 2,000 a month is a hard published number, and it works in editors, the CLI, github.com and mobile. You give up model choice, because Free is Auto only. Pick **Antigravity** for agentic work on Gemini models. It includes parallel agents, subagents, browser use and scheduled tasks at $0, but the weekly quota has no number and Google's own pricing and docs pages disagree on which models are free. Antigravity is the better test bed for multi-agent workflows. Copilot Free is the safer default for a team that mostly wants autocomplete and light chat.

### Kilo Code vs Cline

Both are open source and both live in your editor. Pick **Kilo Code** when you want hosted free models through Auto Free and a path to a subscription with bonus credits. Pick **Cline** when you already have a provider key, or a local model, and want the longest-running open harness (71,891 stars against Kilo's 28,124 on November 25, 2026). Cline's own pages sell it as free software with no hosted free tier of its own; any free-model promotions are described only by Freebuff's comparison post.

## Skills and MCP servers that work with these tools

Eleven of the twelve tools in this post document support for the open SKILL.md format (see the feature matrix; Freebuff is the one I could not verify). That means a skill from the agentskills.codes catalog is not tied to one agent. Free tiers are small, so a focused skill that tells the agent exactly what to do is worth more here than on an unmetered plan. Each entry below was confirmed to return HTTP 200 on November 25, 2026, and the install counts come from the catalog API the same day.

- [code-review](https://agentskills.codes/skills/code-review) (agno-agi, 26 installs): linting, style checks and best-practice review. A cheap way to spend a small allowance on a review pass instead of an open-ended chat.
- [github-code-review](https://agentskills.codes/skills/github-code-review) (ruvnet, 13 installs): GitHub-oriented review with swarm coordination. Useful with Copilot Free, where review features are limited.
- [mcp-builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder) (anthropics, 136 installs): a guide for building MCP servers. The most installed entry I found, and useful for any agent that speaks MCP.
- [browser-automation](https://agentskills.codes/skills/browser-automation) (browserbase, 39 installs): browser tasks through CLI commands. Works well with agents that have no built-in browser.
- [refactor](https://agentskills.codes/skills/refactor) (github, 8 installs): surgical refactors that keep behavior unchanged. Good for short, well-scoped sessions.
- [debugging](https://agentskills.codes/skills/debugging) (mrgoonie, 4 installs): a root-cause debugging framework. Keeps a small-quota agent from guessing.
- [conventional-commit](https://agentskills.codes/skills/conventional-commit) (carbon-design-system, 3 installs): consistent commit messages with project linting rules.

Where each client looks for skills, per its own docs: OpenCode reads `.opencode/skills`, `.claude/skills` and `.agents/skills` (plus global `~/.config/opencode/skills` and `~/.claude/skills`); Antigravity defaults to `.agents/skills`; Copilot reads `.github/skills`, `.claude/skills` and `.agents/skills`; Kiro reads `.kiro/skills/` and `~/.kiro/skills/`. A single project-level `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` is therefore picked up by OpenCode, Antigravity and Copilot at once. Install one like this:

```bash
mkdir -p .agents/skills
curl -s 'https://agentskills.codes/api/v1/skills?q=code-review&limit=5' | head -c 400   # find the id in the JSON
curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/ID -o skill.zip && unzip skill.zip -d .agents/skills/code-review   # ID from the API output
```

For budgeting skills against a quota, the agentskills.codes guides on [Codex usage limits for agent workflows](https://agentskills.codes/blog/codex-usage-limits-agent-workflows) and [Copilot AI Credits for agent skills](https://agentskills.codes/blog/copilot-ai-credits-for-agent-skills) cover two of the allowances in this post, and [best models for agent skills](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-models-for-agent-skills-october-2026) is a routing table by skill type if you are choosing among free models.

