## Common mistakes

- **Treating "free" as stable.** Gemini CLI, Qwen Code and Amazon Q Developer each lost their free hosted tier in 2026. If a workflow depends on one free model, keep a second option configured.
- **Comparing request counts across vendors.** Freebuff counts hours, Kiro counts credits, Copilot counts completions and an unquantified credit pool, and Antigravity counts agent work. There is no common unit, which is why the limits table does not rank by number.
- **Sending private code to a free hosted model.** Kilo says Auto Free may route to providers that log prompts. Freebuff says messages personalize ads and some models may use data for training. OpenCode's Zen page states a data policy per model, and they differ. Read the policy for the model you pick.
- **Believing a third-party limit.** Several numbers in this space come from competitors' comparison posts. This post flags every one that came from Freebuff's own post instead of the vendor it describes.
- **Skipping the version.** OpenCode's docs now carry a V1 and V2 notice, Windsurf's pricing page is branded Devin, and Cursor's plans are under The X Subscription. Check you are reading the docs for the version you run.
- **Ignoring the date.** Antigravity's pricing page says several free models go away on November 30, 2026. Re-check before you plan a project around any free model.

## Setup checklist

1. Decide whether the code can go through a free hosted model. If not, use your own key or a local model with Cline, Kilo Code or Qwen Code.
2. Pick the surface: terminal (Freebuff, OpenCode, Gemini CLI/Antigravity CLI), editor (Kilo Code, Cline, Copilot), or a full IDE (Kiro, Windsurf, Cursor).
3. Check the country: Freebuff's allowance depends on it, and some OpenCode free models are blocked in listed countries.
4. Put shared skills in `.agents/skills` so more than one agent can use them.
5. Open the usage dashboard on day one. Kiro, Copilot, Codex and Antigravity all expose usage somewhere.
6. Note the date next to any free model you rely on, and set a calendar reminder for the vendor's stated end date.

## How we evaluated

I read each vendor's pricing page, docs and repository on November 25, 2026, and recorded exactly what the page said, including where it only said "limited" or "light". For GitHub star counts I called the GitHub API, and for package versions I ran `npm view` on the public packages. I confirmed the YouTube video through YouTube oEmbed, confirmed the four X posts through X's syndication endpoint, and requested every image and link in this post to check for a 200 response. I confirmed each catalog skill and each internal blog post by loading its page.

What I did not do: I did not install these agents or run any of them against a task, so there are no latency, quality or real-world quota measurements here. I did not measure how many prompts a free allowance actually lasts. Where Freebuff's own comparison post is the only source for a competitor's number (Kilo's 200 requests an hour, Cline's promotions, OpenRouter's free limits), I say so in the text. Freebuff's own docs site would not load for me, so its hour allowances rest on its comparison post and homepage. There is no neutral benchmark that compares free tiers, so none is quoted.

## FAQ

### What is the best free AI coding agent in 2026?

It depends on which kind of free you need. Freebuff is the best fit if you want a published daily allowance with no key and no card. OpenCode is the best open-source option with free hosted models. GitHub Copilot Free is the safest big-vendor choice. None of them publishes an independent benchmark, so the ranking is about limits and access, not output quality.

### Is Gemini CLI still free?

No. The Gemini CLI README says there is no free tier or Google AI plans, and that free individual access and Google AI Pro and Ultra moved to Antigravity CLI on June 18, 2026. Gemini CLI itself is still open source under Apache-2.0.

### Is Qwen Code still free?

The software is free and open source (Apache-2.0), but the hosted free sign-in is gone. Qwen's auth docs say Qwen OAuth now needs a Plus or Pro subscription from chat.qwen.ai. You can still use Qwen Code with your own API key or a local model.

### Is Cursor free?

There is a Cursor Free plan at $0 with limited agent and Chat requests and Tab completions on Cursor models (Grok and Composer). Cursor publishes no request count. Third-party models are not included in plan usage. Cursor's docs say the legacy Hobby plan works the same way.

### What is Freebuff?

Freebuff is a free, ad-supported coding agent from the Codebuff team. It runs in the terminal, on desktop, in a browser workspace and on iOS and Android. Per Freebuff's comparison post it has been live since February 2026, and its allowance is up to 30 hours a day in the US, 21 in Canada, the UK and Germany, 12 in France, Spain and Italy, and 5 elsewhere. The agents are Apache-2.0 on GitHub.

### Is there a free AI coding agent for VS Code?

Yes, several. Kilo Code (MIT) and Cline (Apache-2.0) are VS Code extensions. GitHub Copilot Free works in VS Code and other editors with 2,000 completions a month. OpenCode has a VS Code extension that wraps its terminal UI. Kilo Code's Auto Free gives hosted models without a key; Cline needs your own key.

### What is the best free CLI coding agent?

For a hosted free tier, Freebuff, OpenCode and Antigravity CLI (`agy`) are the choices. Gemini CLI and Qwen Code are still good open-source CLIs but need your own key or a paid plan. Codex's CLI starts at Plus on OpenAI's page, and also accepts an API key.

### Do any of these need a credit card?

Freebuff's homepage says "No card, no credits to watch", and Kilo's pricing page says "No hosted credit required". GitHub's plans page carries a "No credit card required" line in the Free block. For OpenCode, Antigravity, Kiro, Codex, Windsurf and Cursor, the pages I read did not say either way, so check at sign-up.

### What happened to Amazon Q Developer's free tier?

AWS blocked new Q Developer Free Tier signups on May 15, 2026 and says IDE plugins and paid subscriptions reach end of support on April 30, 2027. AWS points developers to Kiro, which has its own free tier of 50 credits a month.

### Are free coding agents safe for private code?

It depends on the provider. Kilo warns that Auto Free may route to providers that log prompts and outputs, and tells users not to submit confidential data. Freebuff says messages are used to personalize ads and some models may use data for training. OpenCode lists a policy per free model, with some zero-retention and some retaining data for 30 days. If the code is private, use your own key or a local model.

### Can I use agent skills with a free plan?

Skills are a file format, so the agent's plan matters less than whether the client supports it. OpenCode, Kilo Code, Cline, Antigravity, Kiro, Codex, Cursor, Qwen Code, Gemini CLI and Windsurf's local agent all document skill support. GitHub's docs say Copilot supports skills but do not say whether every plan tier gets them, and Copilot Chat skills in IDEs are marked not available on Free. Browse the [agentskills.codes catalog](https://agentskills.codes/skills) for ready-made ones.

### Why do the numbers differ between Google's pages?

Antigravity's pricing page lists five models on the free plan and marks four of them for removal on November 30, 2026. Its docs plans page names Gemini 4 Hydrogen and Gemini 3.8 Flash for free users. Both are Google's, and I could not tell which is more current, so this post shows both.

### How often do free limits change?

Often. Between the Freebuff comparison post on November 23 and November 25, the sources I read already disagreed on details such as free model lists. Gemini CLI, Qwen Code and Amazon Q each changed in 2026, and OpenCode's free models are described as limited-time. Treat any figure here as valid on November 25, 2026.

